p.src:
The positive statement is more delicate: $\text{perceiver} = \text{perceived}
   \quad \longrightarrow \quad
\text{action without psychological separation}.$ This identity-like expression must be read with care. It does not deny ordinary perception of objects. It points to the absence of a separate psychological observer who stands apart from what is seen and then tries to manipulate it.

